  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1007 PM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the region will dominate the forecast for the \nnext week. Expect little change in the \"rinse-and-repeat\" forecast \nwith slow moving afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping on the higher terrain and a few possibly moving off the \nterrain into the lower valleys. This moisture with pwats around an \ninch is trapped under the high with no flow aloft to push it out of \nthe region. Skies clear overnight under the subsidence of the high to \nallow strong diurnal heating after sunrise leading to cu popping by \nlate morning and conditions becoming convective by about noon. Look \nfor the subsidence under the high to stifle the convection limiting \nchances for strong thunderstorms at least through Tuesday, but, \nbesides the typical lightning threat, the regime favors slow moving \nstorms that threaten possible localized heavy rain that may produce \nflash flooding on recent fire scars and steep terrain. You'll want \nto stay up on the forecast and be aware of your surroundings if \nyou'll be out in the hills this week. \n\nFor the latter half of the week, an upper level trough dips south \nfar enough to bring the right entrance region of a few weak \njetstreaks over the region, especially the northern areas. Though \nthe upper-level dynamics will be weak, this combined with the strong \ndiurnal heating may be enough to kick off a few strong thunderstorms \nwith possible large hail and stronger outflow gusts. Stay tuned for \nmore detailed updates over the next few days. Under this high \npressure over the region, temperatures will stay five to ten degrees \nabove normal for the first half of the week, cooling a few degrees \nlater in the week as the jet dips south.",
